# dbfree/.env.sample — Oracle Database Free two-container POC config. Copy
# to dbfree/.env and edit. This is a FRESH schema, deliberately NOT the same
# as adb/.env — the two toolkits target different container topologies (see
# td-ora-utils/CLAUDE.md and dbfree/README.md), and dbfree/ is fully
# standalone: nothing here reads adb/.env or adb/common.sh at all.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# ORACLE CONTAINER REGISTRY — required (both database/free and
# database/enterprise live behind the same login at
# https://container-registry.oracle.com — accept the licence for whichever
# repo DOCKER_IMAGE below points at). Used under the free OTN Developer
# License Terms: developing/testing/prototyping/demonstrating only, single
# developer / single workstation, no production/business/commercial data
# processing, no redistribution to third parties (see README.md).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
ORACLE_REGISTRY_USER=your-oracle-sso-email@example.com
ORACLE_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_oracle_registry_token

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# DATABASE IMAGE — pulled from container-registry.oracle.com (OTN registry
# login required — see above). Do not use :latest — pin an explicit tag and
# verify it exists first (a moving tag is also unnecessary here: Free's
# release cadence means today's :latest always has an equivalent pinned
# version tag, confirmed by comparing digests).
#
# Architecture-specific images — setup-for-dbfree.sh/run-dbfree.sh pick the
# correct one automatically via select_docker_image() (adb/common.sh, same
# helper ../adb/ itself uses) based on detected host arch (arm64/aarch64 ->
# ARM, x86_64 -> AMD; on macOS with Colima, COLIMA_ARCH in adb/.env overrides
# for x86_64 emulation the same way it does for ../adb/).
#
# Plain Database Free rather than ADB-Free or Enterprise Edition:
#   - ADB-Free hit ORA-04036 (PGA_AGGREGATE_LIMIT exceeded) against
#     caseweave's vector-embedding workers, and its autonomous management
#     layer BLOCKS the fix: `ALTER SYSTEM SET pga_aggregate_limit=...` fails
#     with ORA-01031 (insufficient privileges) even as ADMIN/DBA — confirmed
#     empirically, this is not adjustable on ADB-Free at any privilege level.
#   - Plain Database Free shares the same 2GB-default pga_aggregate_limit,
#     but — unlike ADB-Free — it's an ordinary DBA-adjustable init
#     parameter: `ALTER SYSTEM SET pga_aggregate_limit=<n>G SCOPE=BOTH`
#     works and takes effect immediately (confirmed empirically). run-dbfree.sh
#     raises it automatically via DB_PGA_AGGREGATE_LIMIT below.
#   - Oracle publishes native arm64 tags (`-arm64` suffix) for Database Free
#     but NOT for Enterprise Edition (verified against the registry's own
#     tag list and manifests — EE is amd64-only, no multi-arch manifest at
#     any tag). On Apple Silicon this means Free runs at native speed;
#     Enterprise Edition would need QEMU emulation under Colima.
# To use Enterprise Edition instead (loses the DBA-adjustable PGA limit
# above — see README.md), point both keys at
# container-registry.oracle.com/database/enterprise:<tag> (amd64 only; also
# set ORACLE_PDB=ORCLPDB1 below instead of FREEPDB1).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
DOCKER_IMAGE_ARM=container-registry.oracle.com/database/free:23.26.3.0-arm64
DOCKER_IMAGE_AMD=container-registry.oracle.com/database/free:23.26.3.0-amd64

# Raised from Database Free's 2G default at run-dbfree.sh startup via
# ALTER SYSTEM (SYS, not the ADMIN compat user — see run-dbfree.sh) — this is
# the specific fix ADB-Free could not provide (see DOCKER_IMAGE_ARM/AMD
# comment above). Size relative to the Colima VM's total memory
# (COLIMA_MEMORY in adb/.env, or Docker Desktop/Linux host RAM) — leave
# headroom for the OS, APEX/ORDS, and Ollama itself; do not set this
# anywhere near total host RAM.
DB_PGA_AGGREGATE_LIMIT=3G

# No official ORDS container image is used here (it's also gated behind the
# same OTN registry login as the DB images) — ORDS runs standalone from a
# plain zip download (see setup-for-dbfree.sh's download_ords()) inside a generic, unauthenticated
# JRE base image instead.
ORDS_JAVA_IMAGE=eclipse-temurin:21-jre-jammy
ORDS_DOWNLOAD_URL=https://download.oracle.com/otn_software/java/ords/ords-latest.zip
ORDS_INSTALL_DIR=./ords-install
ORDS_CACHE_DIR=~/.cache/oracle-ords

CONTAINER_NAME=caseweave-dbfree-db
ORDS_CONTAINER_NAME=caseweave-dbfree-ords
DB_HOSTNAME=dbfree.local

# Host-side SQL*Net / EM Express ports. NOT 1521 — confirmed empirically on
# macOS/Colima that a host port held by a long-running container (adb-free,
# in this case, for several weeks) can end up with a stuck NAT/routing state
# on the macOS side even after that container stops AND after a full Colima
# VM restart: raw TCP connects fine (nc -zv succeeds) but no payload ever
# reaches the container. Moving off that port fixed it immediately. If
# SQL*Net connections mysteriously hang here, suspect this and move
# DB_HOST_PORT off whatever port the last long-running container held.
DB_HOST_PORT=15216
EM_EXPRESS_HOST_PORT=5500

# Sets the DB password on first boot (mapped to ORACLE_PWD in
# docker-compose.yml), and APEX_PWD used by ORDS to complete the APEX install.
ORACLE_PWD=CHANGE_ME_choose_a_strong_password

# Default PDB name on official Oracle Database images (Free and Enterprise
# Edition. Plain Database Free (the default DOCKER_IMAGE here) instead uses
# FREEPDB1 — confirmed empirically (v$pdbs) and by the ORA-65011/ORA-14696
# errors that resulted from leaving this at ORCLPDB1 against a Free image.
# Not configurable without also changing docker-compose.yml. Set this back
# to ORCLPDB1 if DOCKER_IMAGE points at an Enterprise Edition image instead.
ORACLE_PDB=FREEPDB1

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# HOST DIRECTORIES — created by setup-for-dbfree.sh. Paths are resolved relative
# to this directory and validated to stay under it before any cleanup step
# (dbfree/run-dbfree.sh -c) is allowed to rm -rf them.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
DB_DATA_DIR=./oradata
ORDS_CONFIG_DIR=./ords_config
APEX_INSTALL_DIR=./apex-install

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# APEX / ORDS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOT 8080 — same stuck macOS-side host-port NAT/routing issue as
# DB_HOST_PORT above can hit any host port that's seen heavy prior use;
# confirmed empirically for both 1521 and 8080 on this Mac. If ORDS never
# answers here (curl hangs, times out), move this off whatever port a
# long-running prior service held.
APEX_PORT=8092
APEX_VERSION=26.1
APEX_DOWNLOAD_URL=https://download.oracle.com/otn_software/apex/apex_26.1_en.zip
# Cache dir so repeated full-cycle test runs don't re-download the zip every
# time — only the extracted copy under APEX_INSTALL_DIR is wiped by cleanup.
APEX_CACHE_DIR=~/.cache/oracle-apex

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# ORACLE INSTANT CLIENT — installed by ./setup-for-dbfree.sh (via
# install-instant-client.sh) into ~/oraclient/<INSTANT_CLIENT>, needed for
# sqlplus (used throughout run-dbfree.sh/load-apex-app.sh). Idempotent — if
# this directory already exists (e.g. from an earlier adb/ setup on the same
# machine, which uses the identical ~/oraclient layout), install is skipped.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Linux x86_64
BASIC_ZIP=instantclient-basic-linux.x64-23.6.0.24.10.zip
SQLPLUS_ZIP=instantclient-sqlplus-linux.x64-23.6.0.24.10.zip
BASIC_URL=https://download.oracle.com/otn_software/linux/instantclient/2360000/instantclient-basic-linux.x64-23.6.0.24.10.zip
SQLPLUS_URL=https://download.oracle.com/otn_software/linux/instantclient/2360000/instantclient-sqlplus-linux.x64-23.6.0.24.10.zip
INSTANT_CLIENT=instantclient_23_6

# macOS Apple Silicon (arm64: M1/M2/M3/M4)
BASIC_ZIP_MAC=instantclient-basic-macos.arm64-23.26.1.0.0.dmg
SQLPLUS_ZIP_MAC=instantclient-sqlplus-macos.arm64-23.26.1.0.0.dmg
BASIC_URL_MAC=https://download.oracle.com/otn_software/mac/instantclient/2326100/instantclient-basic-macos.arm64-23.26.1.0.0.dmg
SQLPLUS_URL_MAC=https://download.oracle.com/otn_software/mac/instantclient/2326100/instantclient-sqlplus-macos.arm64-23.26.1.0.0.dmg
INSTANT_CLIENT_MAC=instantclient_23_26

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# COMPATIBILITY ADMIN USER / load-apex-app.sh password
# Plain Oracle Database Free has no ADMIN user by default — load-apex-app.sh
# (this directory) assumes one exists (it does on ADB-Free).
# create-admin-compat-user.sql.tpl mints a real DBA-privileged ADMIN user
# here so that script works unmodified. This ONE password is used both to
# create/authenticate that ADMIN user and by load-apex-app.sh's own admin
# connection — deliberately a single key, not two that have to be kept in
# sync by hand (an earlier version of this toolkit had that exact footgun:
# a separate ADMIN_COMPAT_PASSWORD here that had to exactly match a
# DEFAULT_PASSWORD in a different file, with no automatic enforcement).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
DEFAULT_PASSWORD=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# OLLAMA (host) — no proxy container needed here: unlike ADB-Free, plain
# Database Free/Enterprise Edition does not force outbound-HTTPS-only, so
# UTL_HTTP can call the host directly.
#
# LINUX HOSTS ONLY (typically hardened cloud VMs — NOT macOS/Colima): if the
# host firewall has a default-deny iptables INPUT chain, the DB container's
# calls to Ollama will fail with ORA-29273 even though Ollama itself is
# running fine — this is a host firewall rule, not an Ollama problem.
# run-dbfree.sh detects this and prints the exact `sudo iptables ...` command to
# run by hand. Set ALLOW_FIREWALL_AUTOFIX=true here to have it apply that
# fix automatically instead (needs root or passwordless sudo) — left false
# by default since modifying host firewall rules is a persistent, host-level
# change that shouldn't happen silently on a host that's never seen this
# before.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434
OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.2:3b
ALLOW_FIREWALL_AUTOFIX=false

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# TIMEOUTS (seconds) — generous enough to cover a cold image pull plus first
# boot / first APEX install. Used by run-dbfree.sh and the full-cycle test.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
DB_HEALTHY_TIMEOUT=900
ORDS_HEALTHY_TIMEOUT=600

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# APEX APP DEPLOYMENT — read by load-apex-app.sh (this directory), not by
# run-dbfree.sh itself. These are application-specific — leave the plain
# keys blank to make -f/-v mandatory on the CLI instead of silently
# defaulting; any key may have a _MAC variant for Apple Silicon-specific
# paths/models (same platform_val() pattern as elsewhere in this file).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
APEX_USER=TRACKER1
APEX_PASSWORD=
APEX_EXPORT_FILE=
APEX_EXPORT_FILE_MAC=
APEX_VECTOR_SQL=
APEX_VECTOR_SQL_MAC=

# LLM_<STATIC_ID>=<url>|<model>|<type> — pre-supplies Generative AI remote
# server URLs for apps whose export marks them p_prompt_on_install=>true.
#   type = local  -> a self-hosted OpenAI-compatible endpoint (Ollama); /v1
#                    is appended automatically, and a workspace credential
#                    is created with a dummy bearer token (Ollama needs no
#                    real auth)
#   type = remote -> URL pre-supplied as-is, no credential created
# Pointed directly at Ollama's host.docker.internal address (NOT an
# ollama-proxy TLS address — that's an ADB-Free-only concept; this DB can
# reach the host directly, see OLLAMA_BASE_URL above). Override any entry
# at import time with load-apex-app.sh's -r STATIC_ID=URL flag instead of
# editing this file, or add your own LLM_<ID> entries for your own app.
LLM_OLLAMA_LOCAL=http://host.docker.internal:11434|llama3.2:3b|local

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# WORKER LLM (optional) — NOT read by anything in this directory. Carried
# here purely as a convenient, documented place for a sibling app's own
# Python/host-side workers to read their LLM settings from, so apps that
# used to read these from adb/.env (the old shared-settings file) have a
# drop-in replacement. See WORKER_LLM_API_STYLE's comment for the protocol
# distinction if you do use these.
#   ollama     -> POST {url}/api/chat            no auth
#   openai     -> POST {url}/v1/chat/completions  Bearer api_key
#   oci_genai  -> OCI Generative AI native        OCI request-signing
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
WORKER_LLM_API_STYLE=ollama
WORKER_LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434
WORKER_LLM_API_KEY=
WORKER_TEXT_MODEL=
WORKER_VISION_MODEL=
WORKER_LLM_OCI_COMPARTMENT_ID=
WORKER_LLM_OCI_PROFILE=DEFAULT
